)=r^{2},\\qquad \\Gamma:\\; (X-N)=R^{2},\n\\]\nwith $r<R$ and $A,B$ their distinct intersection points.  \nDenote by $C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN$ the point nearer $M$ and by $D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN$ the point nearer $N$, so that the points appear on the line $MN$ in the order $C,M,N,D$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Homothety)\n\nThere exists a homothety $h$ with centre $A$ sending $\\Omega$ onto $\\Gamma$.  \n*Proof.*  \nSince $A$ belongs to both circles, the power of $A$ with respect to each circle is $0$.  \nThe line $AN$ meets $\\Omega$ again at a point $A'$; by the intersecting‑chords theorem,\n\\[\nAA'\\cdot AM = AB^{2}=AD\\cdot AN .\n\\]\nHence $AA'/AN = AM/AD$, i.e. the ratio $k:=\\dfrac{AN}{AM}= \\dfrac{AD}{AA'}$ is constant.  \nTherefore the dilation with centre $A$ and factor $k$ sends $M$ to $N$, $C$ to $D$, and maps the whole circle $\\Omega$ onto $\\Gamma$. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Position of $P$)\n\nLet $P$ be the circumcenter of $\\triangle ACD$. Then $P$ lies on the line $AN$ and $AP$ bisects $\\angle CAN$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause $h$ sends $C$ to $D$ and fixes $A$, it sends the circumcircle of $\\triangle ACD$ onto itself; consequently the centre $P$ is invariant under $h$.  \nA point fixed by a homothety with centre $A$ must lie on the line $AN$; thus $P\\in AN$.  \nSince $h$ interchanges $C$ and $D$, the chord $CD$ is mapped onto itself, so $AP$ is the internal angle bisector of $\\angle CAD$, i.e. of $\\angle CAN$. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Points $E$ and $F$)\n\nLet $E\\neq A$ be the second intersection of line $AP$ with $\\Omega$ and $F\\neq A$ the second intersection of $AP$ with $\\Gamma$. Then $AE=AF$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBoth $E$ and $F$ lie on the line $AP$, and $A$ is the centre of the homothety $h$ that sends $\\Omega$ to $\\Gamma$.  \nHence $h(E)=F$ and the similarity