D$ is invariant, and $AP$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle CAD$, i.e. of $\\angle CAN$. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Equality $AE=AF$)\n\n$AE=AF$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBoth $E$ and $F$ lie on the line $AP$. By Lemma&nbsp;1, $h$ sends $\\Omega$ onto $\\Gamma$ and fixes $A$; consequently $h(E)=F$. The similarity ratio of $h$ is $k=AN/AM$, but $A$ is a common point of the two circles, so $k=1$ for the segment $AE$: $AE\\cdot k = AF$. Hence $AE=AF$. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Cyclicity of $B,E,F,P$)\n\nThe quadrilateral $B\\,E\\,F\\,P$ is cyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause $A,B$ are the intersection points of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$, the chord $AB$ subtends equal angles at any point of the two circles:\n\\[\n\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma&nbsp;2, $AP$ bisects $\\angle CAN$, so\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle DAP . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAdding (2) to (1) gives\n\\[\n\\angle ACB + \\angle CAP = \\angle ADB + \\angle DAP .\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side is $\\angle AEB$ (inscribed angle in $\\Omega$ subtended by $AB$), the right\u2011hand side is $\\angle AFB$ (inscribed angle in $\\Gamma$ subtended by $AB$). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB .\n\\]\n\nSince $P$ lies on $AP$, $\\angle AEB = \\angle BEP$ and $\\angle AFB = \\angle BFP$. Therefore\n\\[\n\\angle BEP = \\angle BFP ,\n\\]\nwhich is the criterion for concyclicity of $B,E,F,P$. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (Altitude from $P$)\n\nIn $\\triangle PMN$, the altitude from $P$ is the line $PH$, i.e.\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy definition, the orthocentre $H$ is the intersection of the three altitudes of $\\triangle PMN$. The altitude issued from $P$ is the line through $P$ perpendicular to the opposite side $MN$; therefore $H$ lies on this line, and $PH\\perp MN$. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 6 (The line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is the external symmedian of $\\triangle PBE$)\n\nLet $\\ell$ be the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$. Then $\\ell$ is the external symmedian of $\\triangle PBE$ through the vertex $P$.\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Image of $MN$ under the homothety $h$** \u2013 By Lemma&nbsp;1, $h$ fixes the line $MN$ (it sends $M$ to $N$ and $C$ to $D$, both lying on $MN$). Hence $h$ maps any direction parallel to $MN$ onto a direction still parallel to $MN$.\n\n2. **Image of the altitude $PH$** \u2013 From Lemma&nbsp;5, $PH\\perp MN$. Applying $h$, the image $h(PH)$ is a line through $P$ (because $h$ fixes $P$) that is also perpendicular to $MN$; consequently $h(PH)$ is the reflection of $PH$ about the line $AP$, the axis of the homothety (Lemma&nbsp;2).\n\n3. **Relation with the median of $\\triangle PBE$** \u2013 In the cyclic quadrilateral $B,E,F,P$ (Lemma&nbsp;4) the chord $BE$ subtends equal angles at $P$ and $F$; therefore $PB\\cdot PE = PF^{2}$. By the power\u2011of\u2011a\u2011point theorem applied to $P$ with respect to the circle $(BEF)$, $PF$ is the *symmedian* length from $P$ to side $BE$. More concretely, the line $PF$ is the internal symmedian of $\\triangle PBE$ (the symmedian is the isogonal image of the median). Since $PF$ is the image of the altitude $PH$ under the isogonal conjugation with respect to $\\angle BPE$ (the isogonal of a line perpendicular to $MN$ is the line symmetric to it about the internal bisector $AP$), the line $h(PH)$ coincides with the internal symmedian $PF$.\n\n4. **External symmedian** \u2013 The external symmedian of $\\triangle PBE$ is the isogonal conjugate of the external median, i.e. the reflection of the internal symmedian $PF$ about the external bisector of $\\angle BPE$. Because $AP$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle BPE$ (Lemma&nbsp;2 and the fact that $B,E,F,P$ are concyclic), the line symmetric to $PF$ with respect to