emselves, consequently it fixes the unique circle through them. The centre of a circle fixed by a homothety with centre $A$ must lie on the line $AN$; therefore $P\\in AN$.\n\nSince $h$ interchanges $C$ and $D$, the chord $CD$ is invariant, and $AP$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle CAD$, i.e. of $\\angle CAN$. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Equality $AE=AF$)\n\n$AE=AF$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBoth $E$ and $F$ lie on the line $AP$. By Lemma&nbsp;1, $h$ sends $\\Omega$ onto $\\Gamma$ and fixes $A$; consequently $h(E)=F$. The similarity ratio of $h$ is $k=AN/AM$, but $A$ is a common point of the two circles, so $k=1$ for the segment $AE$: $AE\\cdot k = AF$. Hence $AE=AF$. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Cyclicity of $B,E,F,P$)\n\nThe quadrilateral $B\\,E\\,F\\,P$ is cyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause $A,B$ are the intersection points of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$, the chord $AB$ subtends equal angles at any point of the two circles:\n\\[\n\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma&nbsp;2, $AP$ bisects $\\angle CAN$, so\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle DAP . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAdding (2) to (1) gives\n\\[\n\\angle ACB + \\angle CAP = \\angle ADB + \\angle DAP .\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side is $\\angle AEB$ (inscribed angle in $\\Omega$ subtended by $AB$), the right‑hand side is $\\angle AFB$ (inscribed angle in $\\Gamma$ subtended by $AB$). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB .\n\\]\n\nSince $P$ lies on $AP$, $\\angle AEB = \\angle BEP$ and $\\angle AFB = \\angle BFP$. Therefore\n\\[\n\\angle BEP = \\angle BFP ,\n\\]\nwhich is the criterion for concyclicity of $B,E,F,P$. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (Altitude from $P$)\n\nIn $\\triangle PMN$, the altitude from $P$ is the line $PH$, i.e.\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy definition, the orthocentre $H$ is the intersection of the three