E$. Because $AP$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle BPE$ (Lemma&nbsp;2 and the fact that $B,E,F,P$ are concyclic), the line symmetric to $PF$ with respect to $AP$ is precisely the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$, namely $\\ell$.\n\nThus $\\ell$ is the external symmedian of $\\triangle PBE$ through $P$. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 7 (Tangency)\n\nThe line $\\ell$ (through $H$ parallel to $AP$) is tangent to the circumcircle $\\omega_{BEF}$ of $\\triangle BEF$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFrom Lemma&nbsp;4 the points $B,E,F,P$ lie on the same circle $\\omega_{BEF}$.  \nA classical property of a triangle states: *the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle of the triangle at the point where the symmedian meets the circle*. Applying this to $\\triangle PBE$, whose external symmedian through $P$ is $\\ell$ (Lemma&nbsp;6), we obtain that $\\ell$ touches $\\omega_{BEF}$ at the second intersection of $\\ell$ with the circle. Hence $\\ell$ is tangent to $\\omega_{BEF}$. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final Step\n\nThe line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is exactly the line $\\ell$ considered in Lemma&nbsp;7. Consequently it is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$, which completes the proof. ∎\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAMPLE\n\nFollow the output format specified in your system prompt.\n"
