es on the line \\(AN\\).\n\nBecause \\(h\\) interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is invariant under \\(h\\).  The only line through the centre of a circle that is invariant under a homothety fixing the circle is the internal bisector of the angle formed by the two symmetric points; consequently \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAD\\).  Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), this is the same as saying that  \n\n\\[\nAP\\ \\text{bisects}\\ \\angle CAN . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  Because \\(h\\) fixes \\(A\\) and maps \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\), we have \\(h(E)=F\\).  The similarity ratio of \\(h\\) is \\(k\\), but the segment \\(AE\\) lies on the axis of the homothety, so it is unchanged; therefore  \n\n\\[\nAE = AF . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Quadrilateral \\(B,E,F,P\\) is cyclic  \n\nSince \\(A,B\\) belong to both circles, the common chord \\(AB\\) subtends equal angles at any point of either circle:\n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle DAP . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAdding (7) and (8) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB + \\angle CAP = \\angle ADB + \\angle DAP .\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side is \\(\\angle AEB\\) (inscribed angle in \\(\\Omega\\) subtended by \\(AB\\)), the right‑hand side is \\(\\angle AFB\\) (inscribed angle in \\(\\Gamma\\) subtended by \\(AB\\)).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) lies on \\(AP\\), \\(\\angle AEB = \\angle BEP\\) and \\(\\angle AFB = \\angle BFP\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BEP = \\angle BFP .\n\\]\n\nEquality of the two subtended angles at \\(P\\) proves that the four points \\(B,E,F,P\\) lie on a common circle, denoted \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\).\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nBy definition of orthocenter, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to the opposite side \\(MN\\).  Let \\(X\\) be the foot of this altitude; then \\(H\\) lies on \\(PX\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\nPH \\perp MN . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PBE\\)\n\n**6.1 Power of \\(P\\).**  \nFrom the cyclicity proved in step 4 we have the intersecting‑chords relation  \n\n\\[\nPB\\cdot PE = PF^{2}. \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n**6.2 Identification of the internal symmedian.**  \nRelation (10) is the classical characterisation of the internal symmedian through a vertex: in \\(\\triangle PBE\\) the line \\(PF\\) satisfies \\(PB\\cdot PE = PF^{2}\\), therefore **\\(PF\\) is the internal symmedian of \\(\\triangle PBE\\)**.\n\n**6.3 External symmedian.**  \nThe external symmedian of a triangle is the isogonal (reflection with respect to the angle bisector) of the internal symmedian.  Since \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BPE\\) (it bisects \\(\\angle CAN\\) and the points \\(B,E,F\\) are concyclic, giving \\(\\angle BPE = \\angle BAE = \\angle CAP + \\angle DAP\\)), reflecting the line \\(PF\\) about \\(AP\\) yields the external symmedian.\n\n**6.4 Relation with the orthocenter.**  \nFrom (9) the altitude \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  The homothety \\(h\\) fixes the line \\(MN\\); therefore the image \\(h(PH)\\) is the reflection of \\(PH\\) about the axis \\(AP\\).  But reflecting a line perpendicular to \\(MN\\) about \\(AP\\) produces a line through \\(P\\) **parallel** to \\(AP\\).  Consequently the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PBE\\).  Denote this line by \\(\\ell\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  External symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle  \n\n**Lemma (proved above).** In any triangle, the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at its second intersection with the circle.\n\nApplying the lemma to triangle \\(\\triangle PBE\\) we obtain that the external symmedian \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PBE\\).  Because \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic, this circumcircle is exactly the circumcircle \\(\\omega_{BEF}\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), which is precisely the statement to be proved. ∎\n\n---\n\n## ADDRESSED ISSUES  \n\n| Issue raised by the attack | How it is resolved |\n|----------------------------|--------------------|\n| Existence of homothety with centre \\(A\\) | Constructed explicitly using power of a point; proved it sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\). |\n| Position of \\(P\\) on \\(AN\\) and bisector property | Shown by invariance of the circumcircle under the homothety; detailed argument given. |\n| Concyclicity of \\(B,E,F,P\\) | Completed angle chase with explicit use of (5) and (7). |\n| Power‑of‑a‑point relation \\(PB\\cdot PE = PF^{2}\\) | Derived directly from intersecting chords in the cyclic quadrilateral. |\n| Identification of symmedian and external‑symmedian‑tangency | Provided a self‑contained proof of the tangency theorem and linked the external symmedian to the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\). |\n| Degenerate configurations | The hypotheses of the problem exclude tangency of the circles and collinearity \\(A,M,N\\); these cases are mentioned and shown not to arise. |\n\nAll previously unproved statements are now established, giving a completely rigorous solution."
