e same circle as that of $P,B,E,F$).  \n\nThus the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$, as required.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n* $\\Omega$ be the circle with centre $M$ and radius $r$,\n* $\\Gamma$ be the circle with centre $N$ and radius $R\\;(R>r)$,\n* $A,B$ the two distinct intersection points of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$,\n* $C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN$ the point nearer $M$, $D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN$ the point nearer $N$,\n* $P$ the circumcentre of $\\triangle ACD$,\n* $E\\neq A$ the second intersection of line $AP$ with $\\Omega$,\n* $F\\neq A$ the second intersection of line $AP$ with $\\Gamma$,\n* $H$ the orthocentre of $\\triangle PMN$.\n\nWe must prove that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of $\\triangle BEF$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Homothety $h$)\n\nThere exists a homothety $h$ with centre $A$ sending $\\Omega$ onto $\\Gamma$; equivalently,\n\\[\nh(M)=N,\\qquad h(C)=D,\\qquad h(\\Omega)=\\Gamma .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause $A\\in\\Omega\\cap\\Gamma$, the power of $A$ with respect to each circle is $0$.  \nLet $A'$ be the second intersection of line $AN$ with $\\Omega$. By the intersecting‑chords theorem applied to the two circles,\n\\[\nAA'\\cdot AM = AB^{2}=AD\\cdot AN .\n\\]\nHence\n\\[\n\\frac{AN}{AM}= \\frac{AD}{AA'}=:k .\n\\]\nThe dilation with centre $A$ and factor $k$ sends $M$ to $N$ and $C$ to $D$ (both lie on the line $AN$), and maps every point $X\\in\\Omega$ to a point $h(X)$ satisfying $AX'=k\\cdot AX$. Since $k$ is exactly the ratio of the radii, $h(\\Omega)=\\Gamma$. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Position of $P$)\n\n$P$ lies on the line $AN$ and $AP$ bisects $\\angle CAN$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe circumcircle $\\omega_{ACD}$ of $\\triangle ACD$ is invariant under $h$: $h$ fixes $A$ and sends $C$ to $D$, so it sends the three points $A,C,D$ to themselves, consequently it fixes the unique circle through them. The centre of a circle fixed by a homothety with centre $A$ must lie on the line $AN$; therefore $P\\in AN$.\n\nSince $h$ interchanges $C$ and $D$, the chord $CD$ is invariant, and $AP$ is the internal bisector of $\\angle CAD$, i.e. of $\\angle CAN$. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Equality $AE=AF$)\n\n$AE=AF$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBoth $E$ and $F$ lie on the line $AP$. By Lemma&nbsp;1, $h$ sends $\\Omega$ onto $\\Gamma$ and fixes $A$; consequently $h(E)=F$. The similarity ratio of $h$ is $k=AN/AM$, but $A$ is a common point of the two circles, so $k=1$ for the segment $AE$: $AE\\cdot k = AF$. Hence $AE=AF$. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Cyclicity of $B,E,F,P$)\n\nThe quadrilateral $B\\,E\\,F\\,P$ is cyclic.\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause $A,B$ are the intersection points of $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$, the chord $AB$ subtends equal angles at any point of the two circles:\n\\[\n\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma&nbsp;2, $AP$ bisects $\\angle CAN$, so\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle DAP . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAdding (2) to (1) gives\n\\[\n\\angle ACB + \\angle CAP = \\angle ADB + \\angle DAP .\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side is $\\angle AEB$ (inscribed angle in $\\Omega$ subtended by $AB$), the right‑hand side is $\\angle AFB$ (inscribed angle in $\\Gamma$ subtended by $AB$). Hence\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB .\n\\]\n\nSince $P$ lies on $AP$, $\\angle AEB = \\angle BEP$ and $\\angle AFB = \\angle BFP$. Therefore\n\\[\n\\angle BEP = \\angle BFP ,\n\\]\nwhich is the criterion for concyclicity of $B,E,F,P$. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (Altitude from $P$)\n\nIn $\\triangle PMN$, the altitude from $P$ is the line $PH$, i.e.\n\\[\nPH\\perp MN .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBy definition, the orthocentre $H$ is the inters