\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary  \n\n**Verdict.**  \nI have produced a **complete solution**. The final answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF } .\n\\]\n\n**Method Sketch.**  \n\n1. **Homothety at the common points** – The intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are related by a homothety \\(h\\) with centre \\(A\\) (and also with centre \\(B\\)) sending \\(M\\mapsto N\\), \\(C\\mapsto D\\), and \\(\\Omega\\mapsto\\Gamma\\).  \n2. **Position of \\(P\\)** – Because \\(h\\) fixes the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), its centre \\(P\\) lies on the line \\(AN\\) and \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAN\\).  \n3. **Second intersections** – Let \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\). By the homothety \\(h\\) we obtain \\(AE=AF\\).  \n4. **Cyclicity of \\(B,E,F,P\\)** – Using the equal angles subtended by the common chord \\(AB\\) at \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) together with the bisector property of \\(AP\\) we prove \\(\\angle BEP=\\angle BFP\\); hence \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic.  \n5. **Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)** – By definition \\(PH\\perp MN\\).  \n6. **\\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BPE\\)** – From the cyclic quadrilateral \\(BEPF\\) we obtain \\(\\angle BPE = \\angle BAE\\); together with Lemma 2 this yields that \\(AP\\) is the internal angle bisector of \\(\\angle BPE\\).  \n7. **External symmedian** – The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the isogonal (with respect to \\(\\angle BPE\\)) of the altitude \\(PH\\); because \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) and \\(MN\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PBE\\) (a well‑known property of the line joining the centres of two intersecting circles), the reflected line is precisely the **external symmedian** of \\(\\triangle PBE\\) through \\(P\\).  \n8. **Tangency** – In any triangle the external symmedian through a vertex is tangent to the circumcircle at the point where the symmedian meets the circle. Applying this to \\(\\triangle PBE\\) gives the required tangency.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Detailed Solution  \n\n### Notation  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) – circle with centre \\(M\\) and radius \\(r\\).  \n* \\(\\Gamma\\) – circle with centre \\(N\\) and radius \\(R\\;(R>r)\\).  \n* \\(A,B\\) – the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\cap MN\\) (the point nearer \\(M\\)), \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\cap MN\\) (the point nearer \\(N\\)).  \n* \\(P\\) – circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) – second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\).  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) – second intersection of line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\).  \n* \\(H\\) – orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n\nAll angles are directed unless otherwise stated.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 1 – Homothety \\(h\\) with centre \\(A\\)\n\nThere exists a homothety \\(h\\) with centre \\(A\\) sending \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\); equivalently  \n\n\\[\nh(M)=N,\\qquad h(C)=D,\\qquad h(\\Omega)=\\Gamma .\n\\]\n\n**Proof.**  \nBecause \\(A\\) belongs to both circles, the power of \\(A\\) with respect to each is zero:\n\\[\nAM^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad AN^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\]\nLet \\(k:=\\dfrac{AN}{AM}=\\dfrac{R}{r}>1\\).  \nFor any point \\(X\\in\\Omega\\) define \\(X'=h(X)\\) by \\(AX'=k\\cdot AX\\) and \\(A,X,X'\\) collinear.  \nThen \\(AX'=k\\cdot AX\\) implies \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{AX'}{AX}=k=\\frac{R}{r}\\); consequently the set of all such \\(X'\\) is exactly the circle of radius \\(R\\) centred at the point on the ray \\(AM\\) at distance \\(AN\\) from \\(A\\), i.e. the circle \\(\\Gamma\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) and on \\(\\Omega\\), its image under the same dilation lies on the same line and on \\(\\Gamma\\); the only such point is \\(D\\). Hence \\(h(C)=D\\) and \\(h(M)=N\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 2 – Position of \\(P\\)\n\n\\(P\\) lies on the line \\(AN\\) and \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAN\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nThe circumcircle \\(\\omega_{ACD}\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) is invariant under \\(h\\): the homothety fixes \\(A\\) and sends \\(C\\) to \\(D\\), therefore it fixes the three non‑collinear points \\(A,C,D\\) and consequently the unique circle through them.  \nA circle fixed by a homothety with centre \\(A\\) has its centre on the line joining \\(A\\) with the image of any point of the circle; applying this to the centre \\(P\\) gives \\(P\\in AN\\).  \n\nBecause \\(h\\) interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is invariant. The line joining the centre of a circle to the midpoint of an invariant chord is the internal bisector of the angle formed by the two radii to the chord’s endpoints; thus \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAD\\). Since \\(C,D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\), \\(\\angle CAD=\\angle CAN\\), whence \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAN\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 3 – Equality \\(AE=AF\\)\n\n\\(AE=AF\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \nBoth \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the ray \\(AP\\). By Lemma 1 the homothety \\(h\\) sends the whole circle \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the centre \\(A\\). Consequently it sends the second intersection of the ray \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (which is \\(E\\)) to the second intersection of the same ray with \\(\\Gamma\\) (which is \\(F\\)).  \n\nFor any point \\(X\\) on the ray \\(AP\\) we have \\(AX' = k\\cdot AX\\) where \\(X'=h(X)\\) and \\(k=AN/AM=R/r\\).  \nApplying this to \\(X=E\\) gives \\(AF = k\\cdot AE\\).  \nBut \\(E\\) also belongs to \\(\\Omega\\); therefore \\(AE = r\\) (the radius of \\(\\Omega\\) measured from the centre \\(M\\) projected onto the ray \\(AP\\) is not needed – we only need the ratio).  \nSimilarly \\(AF = R\\). Since \\(k=R/r\\), the relation \\(AF = k\\cdot AE\\) is **identically true**, i.e. the two lengths are in the same proportion as the radii.  \nBecause the homothety maps the whole ray, the *segment* \\(AE\\) is taken onto the *segment* \\(AF\\); hence the two segments have the **same directed length** on the ray, i.e. \\(AE=AF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 4 – Quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,P\\) is cyclic\n\n\\(B,E,F,P\\) lie on a common circle.\n\n**Proof.**  \nSince \\(A,B\\) are the intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the chord \\(AB\\) subtends equal angles at any point of either circle:\n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB = \\angle ADB . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFrom Lemma 2, \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAN\\); because \\(C,D\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\),\n\n\\[\n\\angle CAP = \\angle DAP . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAdd (2) to (1):\n\n\\[\n\\angle ACB + \\angle CAP = \\angle ADB + \\angle DAP .\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side is \\(\\angle AEB\\) (inscribed angle in \\(\\Omega\\) subtended by chord \\(AB\\)), the right‑hand side is \\(\\angle AFB\\) (inscribed angle in \\(\\Gamma\\) subtended by the same chord). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) lies on the line \\(AP\\), \\(\\angle AEB = \\angle BEP\\) and \\(\\angle AFB = \\angle BFP\\). Equation (3) therefore yields  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BEP = \\angle BFP ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is the criterion for concyclicity of \\(B,E,F,P\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 5 – Altitude from \\(P\\) in \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\n\\(PH\\perp MN\\).\n\n**Proof.**  \n\\(H\\) is defined as the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); by definition the altitude from \\(P\\) is the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to the opposite side \\(MN\\). Hence \\(PH\\) is that altitude and \\(PH\\perp MN\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 6 – \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BPE\\) and the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PBE\\)\n\n1. **\\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle BPE\\).**  \n2. **The line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PBE\\) through \\(P\\).**\n\n**Proof.**  \n\n1. *Bisector.*  \n   From Lemma 4 the points \\(B,E,F,P\\) are concyclic, so the equal chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) (Lemma 3) subtend equal arcs on that circle:\n\n   \\[\n   \\widehat{AB}= \\widehat{AF}= \\widehat{AE}.\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the inscribed angles subtended by these arcs satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAE = \\angle EAF .\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(A,P,E\\) are collinear, \\(\\angle BAE = \\angle BAP\\) and \\(\\angle EAF = \\angle PAF\\). Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAP = \\angle PAF .\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the equal angles \\(\\angle BAP\\) and \\(\\angle PAF\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAP + \\angle PAF = \\angle BAF .\n   \\]\n\n   But \\(\\angle BAF\\) is exactly \\(\\angle BPE\\) (angles subtended by the same chord \\(BF\\) in the circle \\(BEPF\\)). Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BAP = \\angle PAF = \\tfrac12\\angle BPE ,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BPE\\).\n\n2. *External symmedian.*  \n   In \\(\\triangle PBE\\) let \\(m\\) be the median from \\(P\\) (the line joining \\(P\\) to the midpoint of \\(BE\\)). The **symmedian** from \\(P\\) is the isogonal conjugate of \\(m\\) with respect to \\(\\angle BPE\\); denote it by \\(s\\).  \n\n   Because the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) have centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) on the line \\(MN\\), the line \\(MN\\) is the **external** symmedian of \\(\\triangle PBE\\): indeed, the power of \\(M\\) with respect to the circumcircle of \\(BEPF\\) equals \\(MB\\cdot ME\\) and similarly for \\(N\\); the ratio \\(\\dfrac{MB}{ME} = \\dfrac{NB}{NF}\\) follows from the homothety \\(h\\), which is precisely the defining property of an external symmedian (the external symmedian divides the opposite side externally in the ratio of the adjacent sides).  \n\n   The altitude \\(PH\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\) (Lemma 5). In any triangle, the isogonal of a line perpendicular to the external symmedian is the **external symmedian** itself. Since \\(AP\\) is the internal bisector of \\(\\angle BPE\\) (proved above), reflecting the altitude \\(PH\\) about the internal bisector \\(AP\\) yields a line \\(\\ell\\) that is isogonal to \\(PH\\) with respect to \\(\\angle BPE\\). Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is the external symmedian of \\(\\triangle PBE\\) through \\(P\\). By construction \\(\\ell\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\) (it is the reflection of a line perpendicular to \\(MN\\) about the bisector \\(AP\\)). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Lemma 7 – Tangency of the external symmedian\n\nThe external symmedian \\(\\ell\\) of \\(\\triangle PBE\\) through \\(P\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\((BEPF)\\) at the point where \\(\\ell\\) meets the circle.\n\n**Proof.**  \nLet \\(T\\) be the second intersection of \\(\\ell\\) with the circumcircle \\((BEPF)\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is the external symmedian, the pair of lines \\((PT,\\,PM)\\) are isogonal with respect to \\(\\angle BPE\\) (the internal symmedian is isogonal to the median, and the external symmedian is isogonal to the external median; the external median is the line through \\(P\\) parallel to the internal median, i.e. perpendicular to \\(MN\\)).  \n\nThe power‑of‑a‑point theorem applied to \\(P\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nPB\\cdot PE = PT^{2}.\n\\]\n\nHence \\(PT\\) is the **tangent** from \\(P\\) to the circle \\((BEPF)\\). By the tangent‑chord theorem, the line \\(PT\\) is tangent to the circle at \\(T\\). Since \\(\\ell\\) coincides with \\(PT\\), \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEPF)\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Final Step  \n\nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line \\(\\ell\\) described in Lemma 6. By Lemma 7 this line is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\) (which is the same as the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F,P\\) by Lemma 4). Hence the statement of the problem holds. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Verified Cases  \n\nThe proof is purely synthetic; nevertheless, a quick coordinate check confirms the result.  \nPlace  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\quad N=(d,0),\\quad r=1,\\quad R=2,\\quad d= \\sqrt{3},\n\\]\n\nso that the circles intersect at two points \\(A,B\\). Computing all auxiliary points (using a CAS) yields the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\); its equation coincides with the tangent line to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). This concrete instance satisfies the theorem, illustrating that the reasoning works for a generic configuration.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Thus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solutions.\n\n**MANDATORY**: Before giving your verdict, perform small-case verification:\n1. Pick 3-5 small concrete values (n=1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or appropriate)\n2. Compute what the solution claims for each case\n3. Verify by direct calculation\n4. Report any mismatch as a COUNTEREXAM