nder the homothety \\(h\\) of Lemma 1, because \\(h\\) fixes the three non‑collinear points \\(A,C,D\\).  A circle fixed by a homothety with centre \\(A\\) has its centre on the line joining \\(A\\) with the image of any point of the circle; applying this to the centre \\(P\\) gives \\(P\\in AN\\).\n\nSince \\(h\\) interchanges \\(C\\) and \\(D\\), the chord \\(CD\\) is invariant.  In a circle, the line joining the centre to the midpoint of an invariant chord is the internal bisector of the angle formed by the two radii to the chord’s endpoints.  Hence \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAD\\).  Because \\(C,D\\) lie on the same line \\(MN\\), \\(\\angle CAD=\\angle CAN\\); therefore \\(AP\\) bisects \\(\\angle CAN\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Power of \\(P\\) with respect to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\))\n\n\\[\nPB\\cdot PE = PC^{2}=PA^{2}=PD^{2}=PF\\cdot PB .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\\(PA=PC=PD\\) because \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(P)=PA^{2}-r^{2}=PC^{2}-r^{2}=PB\\cdot PE ,\n\\]\n\nthe last equality being the power‑of‑a‑point formula for the two intersections of the line \\(PB\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (the points are \\(B\\) and \\(E\\)).  The same computation with \\(\\Gamma\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(P)=PA^{2}-R^{2}=PD^{2}-R^{2}=PB\\cdot PF .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(PA=PC=PD\\), the three expressions are equal, which yields the claimed equalities. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The quadrilateral \\(B\\,E\\,F\\,P\\) is cyclic  \n\nFrom Lemma 3 we have  \n\n\\[\nPB\\cdot PE = PB\\cdot PF .\n\\]\n\nCancelling the non‑zero factor \\(PB\\) gives \\(PE = PF\\); therefore the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(PB\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle BEP = \\angle BFP .\n\\]\n\nEquality of the subtended angles at \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) is the