r:\n\n\\[\nE=\\overline{F}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA direct computation (substituting \\(z=tP\\) into \\(|z-M|=r\\) and solving for the\nreal parameter \\(t\\neq0\\)) yields  \n\n\\[\nE=\\frac{P}{|P|^{2}}\\;,\\qquad F=\\overline{E}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are the inverses of each other with respect to the unit\ncircle centred at the origin.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nFor a triangle with vertices \\(p,m,n\\) the orthocentre is  \n\n\\[\nh=p+m+n-p\\; \\frac{(m-p)(\\overline{n-p})+(n-p)(\\overline{m-p})}\n                {|m-p|^{2}+|n-p|^{2}} .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA much simpler expression is obtained by using the well‑known fact that the\nreflection of the orthocentre in any side lies on the circumcircle of the\ntriangle.  Reflect \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\); the reflected point is the\nantipode of \\(P\\) on the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nH = P + (M+N-2P) = M+N-P .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n(The last equality follows from the vector relation\n\\( \\overrightarrow{PH}= \\overrightarrow{MN}\\).)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\)\n\nFrom (3) the direction of \\(AP\\) is the complex number \\(P\\).  \nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; z = H + sP ,\\qquad s\\in\\mathbb R .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nInsert (7) for \\(H\\) and use (2) for \\(P\\); after a short simplification we\nobtain\n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; z = M+N-P + sP\n        = M+N + (s-1)P .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nBecause of (4)–(5) the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric with respect to the\nreal axis, while \\(B=1\\) lies on that axis.  Therefore the centre of the\ncircumcircle \\((BEF)\\) is the real number  \n\n\\[\nO_{BEF}= \\frac{1+