\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I present a **new proof** of the statement. The previous approach failed because it incorrectly asserted the equality \\(AE=AF\\); a concrete configuration shows that the homothety centred at \\(A\\) sends the whole ray \\(AP\\) to a longer ray, so \\(AF= \\dfrac{R}{r}\\,AE\\neq AE\\). Consequently Lemma 3 and all later arguments that depended on it were invalid.  \n\n**b. Method Sketch** –  \n1. **Inversion at \\(A\\) with power \\(AB^{2}\\).** This inversion interchanges the two given circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) and sends the points \\(C\\leftrightarrow D\\) and \\(E\\leftrightarrow F\\).  \n2. **Image of the configuration.** After inversion the points \\(M,N\\) become the mid‑points of the segments \\(AB\\) and \\(AB\\) respectively, i.e. they both go to the same point \\(O\\) (the centre of the circle with diameter \\(AB\\)). The triangle \\(PMN\\) is sent to a triangle \\(P'OO\\) that is degenerate; the orthocenter \\(H\\) is sent to a point \\(H'\\) that lies on the line through \\(O\\) parallel to the image of \\(AP\\).  \n3. **Reformulation of the required tangency.** Under the inversion the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\) becomes the line \\(BE\\) (because \\(B\\) is fixed and \\(E,F\\) are swapped). Hence the statement “the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\)” is equivalent to “the image line through \\(H'\\) parallel to the image of \\(AP\\) passes through the