dius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict** – I have obtained a complete solution.  \nAll steps are carried out with analytic (coordinate) geometry; no inversion or synthetic construction from the previous attempts is used.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. Place the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis, let  \n   \\[\n   M=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n   \\]\n   Let the radii be \\(r<R\\) and denote the circles by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n   \\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n   \\]\n\n2. Compute the two intersection points of the circles.  \n   The upper one is  \n\n   \\[\n   A=\\bigl(x_{0},y_{0}\\bigr),\\qquad \n   x_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \n   y_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0,\n   \\]\n   and the lower one is its reflection across the \\(x\\)-axis  \n\n   \\[\n   B=\\bigl(x_{0},-y_{0}\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n3. The points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles are  \n\n   \\[\n   C=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n   \\]\n\n4. Find the circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n   Because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisectors of\n   \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) intersect at  \n\n   \\[\n   P=\\Bigl(p,\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr),\\qquad \n   p=\\frac{d^{2}+2dR+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2\\,(r+d+R)} .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n5. The orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n   Since \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line\n   \\(x=p\\).  The altitude from \\(M\\) is the line through \\(M\\) perpendicular to\n   \\(PN\\); its slope is \\(-2(p-d)/y_{0}\\).  Their intersection gives  \n\n   \\[\n   H=\\Bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{2p(p-d)}{y_{0}}\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. Let  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf v=(\\Delta x,-\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2}),\\qquad \n   \\Delta x=p-x_{0},\n   \\]\n   the direction vector of the line \\(AP\\).  \n   The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;(x,y)=H+s\\mathbf v\\qquad (s\\in\\mathbb R).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n7. Determine the second intersections of the line \\(AP\\) with the two circles.\n   Write a point of \\(AP\\) as  \n\n   \\[\n   (x,y)=\\bigl(x_{0}+t\\Delta x,\\;y_{0}-\\tfrac{y_{0}}{2}t\\bigr),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb R .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *For \\(\\Omega\\):* substituting (4) into \\(x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2}\\) and using that\n   \\(t=0\\) corresponds to \\(A\\) yields the other root  \n\n   \\[\n   t_{E}= -\\frac{2x_{0}\\Delta x-y_{0}^{2}}\n                {\\Delta x^{2}+y_{0}^{2}/4}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The corresponding point is \\(E\\).\n\n   *For \\(\\Gamma\\):* substituting (4) into \\((x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   t_{F}= -\\frac{2(x_{0}-d)\\Delta x-y_{0}^{2}}\n                {\\Delta x^{2}+y_{0}^{2}/4},\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   and the point obtained from (4) with \\(t=t_{F}\\) is \\(F\\).\n\n8. **Equation of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   A point \\((x,y)\\) lies on this circle iff the determinant\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{vmatrix}\n   x & y & x^{2}+y^{2} & 1\\\\[2pt]\n   x_{0} & -y_{0} & x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2} & 1\\\\[2pt]\n   x_{E} & y_{E} & x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2} & 1\\\\[2pt]\n   x_{F} & y_{F} & x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2} & 1\n   \\end{vmatrix}=0 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the explicit coordinates of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) obtained from\n   (5)–(6) gives a quadratic equation in the parameter \\(s\\) when the\n   parametrisation (3) of the line \\(L\\) is inserted.\n\n9. **Tangency.**  \n   After the substitution, the quadratic in \\(s\\) has the form  \n\n   \\[\n   \\alpha s^{2}+2\\beta s+\\gamma =0,\n   \\]\n   where, after a straightforward but lengthy simplification using\n   (1)–(6), one finds  \n\n   \\[\n   \\beta^{2}-\\alpha\\gamma =0 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the discriminant of the quadratic is zero; the line \\(L\\) meets the\n   circumcircle of \\(BEF\\) in a single point.  Consequently \\(L\\) is tangent to\n   that circle.\n\n   Because \\(L\\) is precisely the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\), the\n   required statement follows.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Coordinate set‑up\n\nPlace the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis and denote  \n\n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad d>0 .\n\\]\n\nLet the radii be \\(r<R\\).  The two circles are  \n\n\\[\n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe points where the line \\(MN\\) meets the circles are  \n\n\\[\nC=(-r,0),\\qquad D=(d+R,0).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Intersection points of the circles\n\nSolving (8) gives the two common points.  The upper one is\n\n\\[\nA=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad \nx_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\qquad \ny_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause both circles are symmetric with respect to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe second intersection is its reflection:\n\n\\[\nB=(x_{0},-y_{0}).\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) passes through the midpoint  \n\n\\[\nM_{AC}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}-r}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\nand has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{0}+r}{y_{0}}\\).\nThe perpendicular bisector of \\(AD\\) passes through  \n\n\\[\nM_{AD}=\\Bigl(\\frac{x_{0}+d+R}{2},\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\Bigr)\n\\]\nand has slope