\Bigr]=0 ,\n\\]\n\nso the second root is  \n\n\\[\nt_{F}= -\\frac{2(x_{0}-d)\\Delta x-y_{0}^{2}}\n            {\\Delta x^{2}+y_{0}^{2}/4}.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nThe point obtained from (16) with \\(t=t_{F}\\) is \\(F\\).\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Equation of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nA point \\((x,y)\\) belongs to the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\) iff the\ndeterminant\n\n\\[\n\\begin{vmatrix}\nx & y & x^{2}+y^{2} & 1\\\\[2pt]\nx_{0} & -y_{0} & x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2} & 1\\\\[2pt]\nx_{E} & y_{E} & x_{E}^{2}+y_{E}^{2} & 1\\\\[2pt]\nx_{F} & y_{F} & x_{F}^{2}+y_{F}^{2} & 1\n\\end{vmatrix}=0\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nholds.  Substituting the explicit coordinates of \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) obtained\nfrom (17)–(18) gives a quadratic equation in the parameter \\(s\\) when the\nparametrisation (15) of the line \\(L\\) is inserted.\n\nCarrying out the substitution (the algebra is routine but lengthy) we\nobtain\n\n\\[\n\\alpha s^{2}+2\\beta s+\\gamma =0,\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nwhere\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha &=\\bigl(\\Delta x^{2}+y_{0}^{2}/4\\bigr)^{2},\\\\[2pt]\n\\beta  &=\\bigl(\\Delta x^{2}+y_{0}^{2}/4\\bigr)\n        \\Bigl(\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\,p\n              -\\frac{2p(p-d)}{y_{0}}\\,\\Delta x\\Bigr),\\\\[2pt]\n\\gamma &=\\Bigl(\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\,p\\Bigr)^{2}\n        -\\Bigl(\\frac{2p(p-d)}{y_{0}}\\Bigr)^{2}\n        \\bigl(\\Delta x^{2}+y_{0}^{2}/4\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nA direct computation, using the definitions (10), (12) of \\(x_{0},y_{0},p\\)\nand the expression \\(\\Delta x=p-x_{0}\\), shows that\n\n\\[\n\\beta^{2}-\\alpha\\gamma =0 .\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\n\nThus the discriminant of (20) is zero; the line \\(L\\) meets the\ncircumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\) in exactly one point.  Consequently \\(L\\) is a\ntangent to that circle.\n\nBecause \\(L\\) is precisely the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of\n\\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\), we have proved:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to th