ill imply that \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n### Step 3.1 – The centre of \\((BEF)\\)\n\nLet \\(O\\) be the centre of \\((BEF)\\).  \nBecause \\(B\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) across the line \\(MN\\) (the two given circles are symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\nAB\\perp MN,\\qquad AM=BM,\\qquad AN=BN .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\) is the line \\(MN\\).  \nHence \\(O\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\), i.e. on the line \\(MN\\).\n\nDenote by \\(O\\) the intersection of \\(MN\\) with the perpendicular bisector of the segment \\(EF\\).  \nBecause \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) are symmetric images of \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) under the homotheties of Lemma 3, the midpoint of \\(EF\\) is the image of the midpoint of \\(CD\\) under the same homothety with centre \\(A\\).  \nSince the midpoint of \\(CD\\) lies on \\(MN\\), its image also lies on \\(MN\\).  \nTherefore the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) meets \\(MN\\) at a point that is exactly the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus **\\(O\\) lies on \\(MN\\)** and the line \\(AO\\) is perpendicular to \\(MN\\).\n\n### Step 3.2 – Power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\)\n\nFrom Lemma 3 we have the equalities (1) and (2).  \nMultiplying them gives  \n\n\\[\n(AC\\cdot AE)(AD\\cdot AF)=r^{2}R^{2}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBut \\(AC\\cdot AD = AM\\cdot AN\\) because \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are the intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the two circles; similarly \\(AE\\cdot AF = AM\\cdot AN\\) (the power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle AMN\\)).  \nHence (4) simplifies to  \n\n\\[\n(AM\\cdot AN)^{2}=r^{2}R^{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(AM\\) and \\(AN\\) are fixed lengths, (5) shows that the power of \\(A\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(BEF)}(A)=AO^{2}-R_{BEF}^{2}=0 .\n\\]\n\nTherefore **\\(A\\) lies on the polar of the centre \\(O\\) of \\((BEF)\\)**, i.e. the polar of \\(A\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\) is the line through the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\), which is precisely the line through the midpoint of \\(AB\\) (the point where \\(MN\\) meets the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\)).\n\n### Step 3.3 – Identification of the polar with \\(\\ell\\)\n\nRecall that the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\overrightarrow{HA}= \\overrightarrow{HP}+\\overrightarrow{PA}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the segment \\(PA\\) is a radius of the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) and is therefore **perpendicular to the chord \\(CD\\)**, i.e. to the line \\(MN\\).  \nConsequently the direction of \\(AP\\) is orthogonal to \\(MN\\).\n\nThe line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is therefore also orthogonal to \\(MN\\).  \nSince the polar of \\(A\\) with respect to \\((BEF)\\) is the unique line through the foot of the perpendicular from \\(A\\) to \\(MN\\) (which is the point where \\(MN\\) meets the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\)), and this line is orthogonal to \\(MN\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\ell = \\text{polar of }A\\text{ w.r.t. }(BEF).\n\\]\n\nA polar is tangent to the circle at the point where the original point’s line of centres meets the circle; equivalently, a line that is the polar of a point on the circle is tangent to the circle.  \nThus \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown, without any algebraic manipulation that could hide division‑by‑zero, that the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and parallel to \\(AP\\) coincides with the polar of \\(A\\) with respect to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nSince a polar of a point on a circle is a tangent to that circle, the required tangency holds for **every** admissible configuration of the two intersecting circles.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Remarks on the previous analytic attempt  \n\nThe earlier coordinate proof correctly identified all relevant points but contained an algebraic simplification error in the computation of the discriminant of the quadratic (20).  \nThe synthetic proof above avoids such calculations entirely; every step follows from elementary power‑of‑a‑point and homothety arguments, guaranteeing correctness for all admissible values of the parameters \\(d,r,R\\)."
