r with the reasoning.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1 – Coordinate set‑up  \n\n> “Place the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis, let \\(M=(0,0),\\;N=(d,0),\\;d>0\\), and write the two circles as  \n> \\(\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\\) with \\(0<r<R\\).”\n\n**Verification:** Correct. The circles are centred at \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) with the given radii, and the choice of coordinates is a standard, loss‑less reduction.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2 – Intersection points  \n\n> “Solving the system gives the two common points  \n> \\(A=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\;B=(x_{0},-y_{0})\\), where  \n> \\(x_{0}= \\frac{d^{2}+r^{2}-R^{2}}{2d},\\; y_{0}= \\sqrt{r^{2}-x_{0}^{2}}>0\\).”\n\n**Verification:** Correct. Subtracting the two circle equations yields the stated \\(x_{0}\\); substituting back gives \\(y_{0}\\). The positivity of \\(y_{0}\\) follows from the hypothesis that the circles intersect in two distinct points.\n\n---\n\n### Step 3 – Points \\(C,D\\)  \n\n> “The line \\(MN\\) meets the circles at  \n> \\(C=(-r,0),\\;D=(d+R,0)\\).”\n\n**Verification:** Correct. Intersection of the \\(x\\)-axis with \\(\\Omega\\) gives \\(x=\\pm r\\); the order \\(C,M,N,D\\) forces the leftmost point \\((-r,0)\\) for \\(C\\) and the rightmost point \\((d+R,0)\\) for \\(D\\).\n\n---\n\n### Step 4 – Circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\)  \n\n> “Using perpendicular bisectors of \\(AC\\) and \\(AD\\) we obtain  \n> \\(P=\\bigl(p,\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr),\\qquad \n> p=\\frac{d^{2}+2dR+R^{2}-r^{2}}{2\\,(r+d+R)} .\\)”\n\n**Verification:** **Critical Error.**  \nThe circumcenter of a triangle must be equidistant from all three vertices.  \n\n*The correct approach:* Since \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the \\(x\\)-axis, the perpendicular bisector of segment \\(CD\\) is the vertical line  \n\\[\nx = \\frac{-r + (d+R)}{2}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2}.\n\\]\nThe circumcenter must lie on this line; denote its \\(x\\)-coordinate by \\(x_{c}\\).  \nEquating the distances \\(PC = PA\\) yields\n\\[\n(x_{c}+r)^{2}+y^{2}= (x_{c}-x_{0})^{2}+(y-y_{0})^{2},\n\\]\nwhich simplifies (using \\(x_{0}^{2}+y_{0}^{2}=r^{2}\\)) to\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{x_{c}(x_{0}+r)}{y_{0}}.\n\\]\nThus the \\(y\\)-coordinate is **not** universally \\(\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\); it depends on the specific values of \\(d,r,R\\).  \n\nA concrete counter‑example (e.g. \\(d=5,\\;r=3,\\;R=6\\)) shows that the point \\(\\bigl(p,\\frac{y_{0}}{2}\\bigr)\\) is **not** equidistant from \\(A\\), \\(C\\), and \\(D\\). Hence the claimed formula for \\(P\\) is false.\n\nBecause the remainder of the proof (steps 5–11) uses this incorrect point, the logical chain is broken at this stage. No further verification of dependent steps is meaningful.\n\n---\n\n### Step 5 – Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)  \n\n> “Because \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=p\\).  \n> The altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has equation \\(y=-\\dfrac{2(p-d)}{y_{0}}x\\).  \n> Their intersection is \\(H=\\bigl(p,\\;-\\frac{2p(p-d)}{y_{0}}\\bigr).\\)”\n\n**Verification:** **Justification Gap (consequence of Step 4).**  \nThe description of the altitude from \\(P\\) assumes that the \\(x\\)-coordinate of \\(P\\) is \\(p\\) (as given in Step 4) and that the line through \\(P\\) perpendicular to \\(MN\\) is vertical. Even if the line \\(MN\\) is horizontal, the altitude from \\(P\\) is indeed vertical **only if** the foot of the altitude on \\(MN\\) has the same \\(x\\)-coordinate as \\(P\\). This holds when \\(P\\) lies directly above the midpoint of \\(MN\\), which is not guaranteed by the (incorrect) expression for \\(P\\). Consequently the altitude equation and the resulting coordinates of \\(H\\) are not justified.\n\n---\n\n### Steps 6–11 – Direction vector, points \\(E,F\\), circumcircle equation, coefficients \\(\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma\\), discriminant  \n\nAll these steps rely on the coordinates of \\(P\\) and \\(H\\) obtained in the previous (flawed) steps. Because the foundatio